WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.800
From Zombies to presidents, borgs&nbsp;
to cowboys, London to Springfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.194
the best tv cliffhangers know how&nbsp;
to leave our jaws on the floor.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.580
The groundbreaking drama "Lost," a show&nbsp;
famous for its cliffhangers, boasts one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.800
that rises above the rest — "Through the Looking&nbsp;
Glass." A two-part season-ender for Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.420
this episode boasts all the features of a&nbsp;
gut-wrencher: deaths of major characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.902
unresolved fates, and multiple unsolved mysteries,&nbsp;
all designed to leave fans screaming for answers.

00:00:30.902 --> 00:00:33.060
"I'm getting them all off&nbsp;
the island! All of them!"

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.801
"Let me ask you something Jack. Why&nbsp;
do you want to leave the island?"

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:41.340
A signature element of the J.J. Abrams' series&nbsp;
was its use of flashbacks to show the lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.360
of each character before they crashed on the&nbsp;
island. But with "Through the Looking Glass,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.640
the producers introduced something a little&nbsp;
different: flash-forwards! These jumps in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.800
time showed some survivors seemingly living off&nbsp;
the island, as if they had escaped. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.900
back in the present day, a mission to find an&nbsp;
underwater station had sealed Charlie's fate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:06.180
His final, intense message about the boat not&nbsp;
being what it seemed, is unforgettable. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.620
very definition of cliffhanger, finding out&nbsp;
the answers would have to wait for Season 4.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.080
"The Good Place" was a dark, philosophical&nbsp;
comedy about a group of strangers who wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.280
up in the afterlife, which looks a lot&nbsp;
like a pleasant suburb, or an idyllic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.780
utopian village, full of bright colors,&nbsp;
blue skies, and froyo. Their guide through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.400
this new world was a kindly deity named&nbsp;
Michael, charmingly played by Ted Danson.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.360
"You end up spending the rest of&nbsp;
eternity in the real bad place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.360
up to your necks in a volcano full of scorpions."

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:41.820
But early on in the series, Eleanor, played&nbsp;
by Kristin Bell, began figuring out she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.080
actually a bad person when she was alive, and&nbsp;
that she had somehow gotten into the Good Place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.300
by mistake. Eleanor puts all of her efforts into&nbsp;
trying to become a good person so she won't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.280
kicked out, learning about philosophy from her&nbsp;
new friend Chidi. But it doesn't come naturally.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.180
"You guys are so fun. Just&nbsp;
relentlessly fun. I keep thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.420
when are they going to stop being&nbsp;
fun? And the answer is never."

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.380
In Episode 13, however, Eleanor&nbsp;
has a shocking moment of clarity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.920
a revelation that is possibly the&nbsp;
greatest twist in sitcom history:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.862
Eleanor, and her friends Chidi, Tahani, and Jason,&nbsp;
had actually been in the Bad Place all along.

00:02:16.994 --> 00:02:19.034
"THIS is the bad place!"

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.620
Set in Montana's wide-open spaces, "Yellowstone"&nbsp;
is a neo-Western centered on the powerful Dutton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.480
family. Their land — the Yellowstone-Dutton&nbsp;
Ranch — sits at the center of an epic power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.040
struggle between government, business, and&nbsp;
the outlaw spirit. At the end of Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.260
conflicts with a neighboring Reservation,&nbsp;
criminal empires, and rival politicians all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.860
come to a head when multiple assassination&nbsp;
attempts are made on the Duttons.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:51.534
"I'd rather kill a thousand&nbsp;
men than shoot another horse."

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:55.680
Patriarch John, played by Kevin&nbsp;
Costner, is gunned down in the road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.000
multiple gunmen enter the office of his&nbsp;
son, Kayce, and his daughter Beth's fate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.160
is unknown when a giant explosion rocks her&nbsp;
office. These violent attacks left many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.660
the main cast members' fates uncertain. And&nbsp;
because the people behind the assassination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.700
attempt also remained a mystery – this one&nbsp;
counts as a true cliffhanger – with spurs on.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.460
The quintessential '90s sitcom "Friends" is&nbsp;
beloved for many reasons – it's funny, sweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.380
and quirky. But did you know it also featured one&nbsp;
of television's best cliffhangers, centered around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:30.600
Ross and Rachel? The on-again off-again lovebirds,&nbsp;
so winningly played by David Schwimmer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.640
Jennifer Aniston, are definitely off-again when&nbsp;
Ross gets engaged to his new girlfriend, Emily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:41.400
who is British. This engagement leads to the&nbsp;
Season 4 finale, "The One with Ross' Wedding," set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.500
in London. Besides the fact that Rachel decides to&nbsp;
skip the ceremony, everything is going smoothly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.600
That is until Rachel changes her mind, deciding&nbsp;
at the last minute that she still loves Ross and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.501
must stop him from saying his vows. She gets onto&nbsp;
an airplane and tells everyone what she's up to.

00:03:56.823 --> 00:04:01.383
"I'm going to London to tell&nbsp;
this guy that I love him."

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:09.000
But when Rachel sees Ross' happiness, she does&nbsp;
the right thing, backing away from her big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.220
plan. Things don't end there, because when Ross&nbsp;
accidentally says Rachel's name on the altar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.760
everything changes, raising big&nbsp;
questions about Ross' marriage.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.080
"I, Ross."

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:20.880
"Take thee, Emily."

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:21.900
"Take thee, Rachel."

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.140
This little Freudian slip had big implications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.740
for the future of the series. "The&nbsp;
One With the Cliffhanger," indeed!

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:35.280
The prime-time 80s soap "Dallas" chronicled the&nbsp;
power struggles between two rival Texas families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.960
with heaping helpings of scandal, sex,&nbsp;
and murder. Led by actors Larry Hagman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:45.300
Patrick Duffy, Victoria Principal, and Linda&nbsp;
Gray, the most dramatic and scandalous moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.601
was without a doubt the third season&nbsp;
cliffhanger episode – "A House Divided."

00:04:49.601 --> 00:04:51.886
"Who's there?"

00:04:51.886 --> 00:04:57.180
The iconic cliffhanger sets up Hagman's character,&nbsp;
J.R. Ewing, working late at the office, alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.280
Hearing a noise, he walks out into the hall,&nbsp;
where he is gunned down in cold blood. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.620
J.R. was then hospitalized and unable to ID the&nbsp;
gunman, it was left up to the audience to wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:13.200
"Who Shot J.R.?" From news reports to magazine&nbsp;
covers, it was the question on everyone's minds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.880
and a landmark moment in both prime-time&nbsp;
viewing and television marketing. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.446
while the show may feel a bit dated today, "Who&nbsp;
Shot JR?" truly remains a television milestone.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:29.880
"Who Shot Mr. Burns?" was the Simpson's answer to&nbsp;
the famous Dallas finale. But more than a homage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:35.160
to the soapy Texas cliffhanger, "The Simpsons"&nbsp;
final episode of season six was a landmark event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.800
on its own. Fox even aired a prime-time special&nbsp;
called "Springfield's Most Wanted" — hosted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.960
John Walsh of "America's Most Wanted" — complete&nbsp;
with a contest where fans could dial a 1-800&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.160
number to guess who shot Springfield's&nbsp;
nuclear power-plant owner, Mr. Burns.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.720
"Join us as we attempt to solve&nbsp;
the crime of the century. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.800
John Walsh and this is Springfields Most Wanted."

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.980
Like J.R., Mr. Burns had many enemies. Also&nbsp;
like J.R., he was shot by an unseen assassin.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.480
"I don't think we'll ever know who did&nbsp;
this. Everyone in town's a suspect."

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.620
To help viewers to figure out the answer,&nbsp;
clues were planted all over the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:18.918
helping to make "Who Shot Mr. Burns?" one of&nbsp;
the most-watched Simpsons episodes of all time.

00:06:18.918 --> 00:06:21.258
"Coincidence? I don't think so."

00:06:21.258 --> 00:06:23.940
Arriving at the height of&nbsp;
the show's early popularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:28.440
the episode was a smash hit. To this&nbsp;
day, few family series have been able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.980
to equal the levels of excitement&nbsp;
surrounding "Who Shot Mr. Burns?"

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:36.834
"Well I couldn't possibly&nbsp;
solve this mystery. Can you?"

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.540
The trials and tribulations of Walter White&nbsp;
kept viewers on the edge of their sofas for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:48.240
five astounding seasons. White, played by Bryan&nbsp;
Cranston, took us on a rollercoaster ride that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.940
only got more intense as time went on. From a&nbsp;
cancer diagnosis to a life of crime, from wimpy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.220
high school teacher to terrifyingly ruthless and&nbsp;
powerful drug lord, White ends up fighting off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:04.080
an array of enemies. Including the DEA agent&nbsp;
Hank Schrader — who also happened to be his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.360
brother-in-law. Always full of twists and turns,&nbsp;
the mid-season finale of the show's final year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:15.240
"Gliding Over All," exposed the biggest revelation&nbsp;
yet. Showing Shrader finally putting the pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.280
together and figuring out who Walter White&nbsp;
really was. In classic "Breaking Bad" fashion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.380
the climactic scene comes with a humorous twist&nbsp;
– the light bulb moment happens on the toilet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:30.120
After years of coming close to being caught,&nbsp;
it seemed as if Walt's time was finally up.

00:07:30.647 --> 00:07:31.565
"Ha…

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:34.661
Ya' got me." 

00:07:34.661 --> 00:07:37.740
But fans would have to wait almost a year to find out the answer.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:44.460
Created by Hollywood screenwriter Aaron Sorkin and&nbsp;
starring Martin Sheen as President Jed Bartlet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.700
"The West Wing" followed the ups&nbsp;
and downs of life inside the White&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.540
House. Receiving rave reviews for its&nbsp;
high drama and complex characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.740
and practically patenting the fast-paced&nbsp;
walk and talk. Something that's now been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:00.000
parodied multiple times, which as we all&nbsp;
know, is the sincerest form of flattery.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.020
"And how much is Curtis charging the federal&nbsp;
government to fix the wobbly wheel on my chair?"

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:05.700
"He said he'd have to take a look at the jump."

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:06.960
"At his shop."

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:07.620
"Yeah."

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:12.720
The first season went from compelling political&nbsp;
drama to nail-biting thriller, culminating in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.040
season one's dramatic cliffhanger finale – an&nbsp;
apparent assassination attempt on President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.380
Bartlet. The bulk of the episode, mainly involving&nbsp;
Bartlet's preparations for an upcoming town hall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:27.660
doesn't hint at the chaos to come. Making it that&nbsp;
much more shocking, when, at the episode's end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.520
a shooter points his gun at the crowd and fires.&nbsp;
What made it a cliffhanger was how the viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:37.560
couldn't see who was hit. Would Sheen be exiting&nbsp;
the series? Or would Vice President Hoynes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:42.060
played by Tim Matheson, step into the&nbsp;
lead role? All options were on the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:47.042
fan theories were brewing, and the series&nbsp;
was already turning into a beloved classic.

00:08:47.701 --> 00:08:52.380
"Star Trek: The Next Generation" aired&nbsp;
at a time when season-ending cliffhangers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.520
were still a relative rarity. But all of&nbsp;
that changed with this landmark episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.900
capping off season three. Often credited&nbsp;
as the year the show came into its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.040
this episode, titled "The Best of Both&nbsp;
Worlds," put the crew of the Enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.468
in serious peril. The action began with the&nbsp;
Borg attempting a takeover of the Federation.

00:09:10.468 --> 00:09:12.600
"You have committed acts of aggression against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.890
United Federation of Planets! If&nbsp;
you do not withdraw immediately…"

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:18.534
"You will surrender yourself&nbsp;
or we will destroy your ship."

00:09:18.534 --> 00:09:23.460
Later, Captain Picard, played by Patrick&nbsp;
Stewart, is taken captive and transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:29.280
into a Borg. In the episode's final moments,&nbsp;
Picard, as a Borg, proclaims in a monotonous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.440
robotic voice, that he is their new leader.&nbsp;
Commander Riker, played by Jonathan Frakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.460
has a weapon that could wipe out the&nbsp;
Borg ship, but firing it would also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:43.320
mean killing Picard. With no other choice,&nbsp;
and to the dismay of the crew, Riker fires.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.880
"Mr. Warf, FIRE."

00:09:48.627 --> 00:09:53.460
The three little words that followed – "To&nbsp;
Be Continued" – pretty much defined the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:58.680
"cliffhanger." Was Picard dead or alive? Fans&nbsp;
would have to wait three long months to find out.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.840
"The Walking Dead" follows a band of&nbsp;
survivors struggling against the undead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.460
in a zombie apocalypse. Led by former&nbsp;
sheriff Rick Grimes, played by British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:12.900
actor Andrew Lincoln. Season 6 found them&nbsp;
fighting one human villain after another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:17.880
with the worst of all being a man named&nbsp;
Negan. Fiercely portrayed by Jeffrey Dean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:22.680
Morgan , Negan was the most diabolical&nbsp;
adversary the group had ever faced.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.740
"Gonna be pee pee pants city here real soon."

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:29.400
Negan is not even seen until&nbsp;
the devastating season finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:34.680
"The Last Day on Earth." And his emergence from&nbsp;
a battered RV, wielding a barbed wire-wrapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:39.300
baseball bat,as the group kneels on the ground&nbsp;
in front of him, is a truly terrifying reveal.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:47.668
"You can breathe, you can blink, you can cry.&nbsp;
Hell, you're all going to be doing that."

00:10:47.668 --> 00:10:52.560
Negan picks his target and savagely beats them&nbsp;
to death in one of the most violent scenes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:57.720
the series. He even beats the cameraman, making&nbsp;
the final moments a black screen with the sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:01.560
of the bat hitting flesh. In what many&nbsp;
consider to be the ultimate cliffhanger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:05.940
audiences then had to wait until Season&nbsp;
7 to find out who the victim was.

00:11:06.862 --> 00:11:12.360
"Game of Thrones," based on the books by George&nbsp;
R.R. Martin, boasted Shakespearean levels of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:17.640
drama and intrigue, Tolkien-esque world-building&nbsp;
and cutting-edge visual effects. An overnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:23.340
sensation, its season-ending jaw-droppers were&nbsp;
a big part of what kept viewers obsessed. As a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.940
series known for being unafraid to kill&nbsp;
off even its most central characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:32.811
the Season 5 finale titled "Mother's Mercy" had&nbsp;
fans bracing themselves for anything to happen.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:37.200
While readers of the books may have seen it&nbsp;
coming, few others could have anticipated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.760
the shocking death of the series' most&nbsp;
beloved character — Jon Snow. And though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:45.780
he would indeed be resurrected almost&nbsp;
immediately in Season 6's second episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:51.300
his death was devastating, and fans of the series&nbsp;
had to wait nearly a year to see it all play out.

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:57.960
Few shows can lay claim to terms like "landmark"&nbsp;
and "unprecedented" – but David Lynch's "Twin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:03.360
Peaks" certainly earned these descriptors –&nbsp;
and then some. Debuting in 1990, it was unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.460
anything mainstream television audiences had ever&nbsp;
seen. Critics and viewers could hardly find words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.800
to describe the avant-garde thriller about the&nbsp;
murder of a young woman, Laura Palmer, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:19.500
by Sheryl Lee, and the FBI agent assigned to her&nbsp;
case, Kyle MacLachlan's iconic, coffee-and-pie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:25.020
loving Dale Cooper. Filled with incomprehensible&nbsp;
imagery, oddball characters, and a wandering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:29.520
often bizarre plot, viewers were sometimes left&nbsp;
struggling to figure out what it all meant.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.800
"What did you see that night, the&nbsp;
night Laura Palmer was killed?"

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:39.107
"Shh. I'll do the talking."

00:12:39.107 --> 00:12:44.160
And the first season finale was no different.&nbsp;
Titled "The Final Evening," the episode had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:49.380
plenty of action but little resolution. Characters&nbsp;
are clubbed, smothered, and kidnapped. Buildings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:54.840
are set on fire. Laura Palmer's killer is still&nbsp;
unknown. But the biggest shocker happens at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.840 --> 00:13:00.000
end, when Agent Cooper is shot in the stomach&nbsp;
by an unseen assailant. The heart-stopping final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.580
scene left audiences anxious for a conclusion they&nbsp;
would be forced to wait for – an agonizing process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.900
but for a show as strangely compelling&nbsp;
as "Twin Peaks," it was well worth it.

